Scanning electron microscopy. SEM-EDS data were collected using ZEISS Sigma 500 VP Scanning electron microscope equipped with an Oxford Aztec X-EDS detector. Samples were dispersed in ethanol, and drop cast on TEM grids (Ted Pella Inc 200 mesh carbon film copper grids Catalog No. NC0733370). The TEM grids were dried under ambient conditions before SEM-EDS studies. An STEM sample holder was used to mount the TEM grids. Secondary electron images and SEM-EDS data of the samples were collected at a working distance of $8.5\mathrm{mm}$ and an accelerating voltage of $20\mathrm{kV}$ . The EDS was acquired using 1024 channels from 0 to $20\mathrm{keV}$ . EDS maps were collected for 10-15 minutes with a pixel dwell time of 100 µs and a pixel resolution of $1024\mathrm{x}1024.$ . EDS maps and line-scans for zirconium, hafnium and iodine were generated using the $\mathrm{ZrLal}$ line intensity at $2.0\mathrm{keV}_{\cdot}$ , Hf Ma1 line intensity at $1.6\mathrm{keV}$ and I La1 line intensity at $3.9\mathrm{keV}_{\cdot}$ , respectively.
